Hi Alexander

Thanks, yes I have been studying - like some crazed mad man - the Renjin documentation.

To give you more insight into my use-case - I have been given the task of incorporating several r scripts into a java application. Currently these scripts read sample data from CSV files, does various cryptic calculations, then spits out a result.

I should avoid changing the r scripts as much as possible. So I am trying to define domain specific 'contracts' for how R and Java can communicate, being that the input and output data structures will not change. Its really only the R calculations that will change.

Do you have any recommendations on how to translate to/from java lists, for example List<Person> to r data frames -with the goal of have clearly defined boundaries of how java can take and integrate R.

So, in order to find that mapping to/from, I am having to understand r - and this is what is really slowing things down. I am aware of functional languages, but honestly, its unreadable to me. I appreciate that its more consice - but, for me, in doing so, it loses readability - Anyway....

What is your recommended approach - or even better - are there any examples of doing custom Renjin types, types that implement the SEXP interface for example - to help to get a clearer understanding of how to do tranformers.

I think this would not just help me, but I imagine there will be many others who come to Renjin because they need to integrate R - and this is really exactly that. I dont have time to learn R. And our R data scientist was not hired to become a Java programmer. We need to define clear boundaries to integration. And I am thinking that consists of Renjin types that convert from/to specific r types.

If you can give some pointers on how to do that it would really help.

Thanks and regards

