Anton,

Actually there seems to be a bug in the read.csv method and not actually in converting strings to dates.  I was able to read the csv file that you sent in another post.  However, I had to read it with

data = read.csv("org.csv", header = false, sep = ";").

If you then remove the first line, you can continue with your script and convert the string into date.  The error you are getting about range [1, 23] is that when we read.csv and remove the header, the date only has the year and not the rest of the string and converting that crashes.

I haven´t finished converting your script to Renjin (not enough time to play with it)... but here what I got in SciCom.  Could have done it with here doc probably.  Can try it another time....

=====

require 'scicom'

# read the data.  Bug in read__table and read__csv... doesn't read properly if header is true
data0 = R.read__table("org.csv", header: false, sep: ";")

# remove the first line that has the header
data0 = data0[-1, nil]

# add headers again
data0.attr.names = R.c("id", "operation", "orderOpenPrice", "orderClosePrice", "orderOpenTime", "orderCloseTime",
  "orderProfit", "orderSwap", "symbol", "orderTicket", "volume", "status")

# order by close time
data0.orderCloseTime = R.strptime(data0.orderCloseTime, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
data0.orderOpenTime = R.strptime(data0.orderOpenTime, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
data1 = data0[R.order(data0.orderCloseTime), nil]

deposit = 500_005

======

